“Everyone experiences grief differently, Dad.”
We were in the medical suite where my mother had taken her last

breath. While we were waiting for paperwork and for the body to be released, my father had devised a plan to send me away to boarding school. Boarding school was a fancy term for a mental institution, and knowing my father, probably the worst of its kind. However, Damon insisted on going with me. Joe lost it at that suggestion. For one, he didn’t want to send away his heir. Secondly, Joe wanted to ship me off to a place grimmer than a mental asylum, where I would never see the light of day again. Joe’s hands would be tied if Damon joined me, and he would have to send us to some fancy school in Switzerland instead. Damon knew it, too. He was diplomatic, not obtuse.
